# Files that MUST stay LF regardless of the committer's core.autocrlf.
#
# A dpkg maintainer script with CRLF endings has a shebang of `#!/bin/sh\r`,
# which the kernel resolves to a nonexistent interpreter — the package then
# fails to configure on EVERY host, and for a self-updating fleet that is a
# fleet-wide broken upgrade. This repo is developed from a Windows box where
# `core.autocrlf=true`, so the normalisation currently happens by config
# rather than by rule; this makes it a rule.
agents/roomlerd/packaging/linux/debian/* text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, lower stakes: systemd refuses to parse a unit whose lines
# end in CR, and the shell scripts are run on Linux hosts.
agents/roomlerd/packaging/linux/*.service text eol=lf
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf

# The sanitisation guard runs on Linux CI; a CRLF shebang would break it.
.claude/skills/sanitize-hostnames/*.sh text eol=lf
.claude/skills/sanitize-hostnames/*.py text eol=lf

# FR-62 A2: the vendored-FFmpeg source patches in .github/ffmpeg-patches/ are
# applied verbatim by the vendor builders (`git apply -p1` on the Linux/Windows
# runners, the vcpkg overlay port's PATCHES on Windows) against LF-normalised
# FFmpeg sources. They MUST stay LF — a CRLF-converted patch fails to apply and
# would inject CR into nvenc.c — and a unified diff's context line for a blank
# source line is legitimately a single space, so the trailing-whitespace check
# must not fire on them.
.github/ffmpeg-patches/*.patch text eol=lf whitespace=-blank-at-eol
